    "prompt": "If \\(9^{x+2}=240+9^x,\\) then the value of \\(x\\) is:"
  },
  "schemaVersion": "3.0",
  "solutions": [
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "math:real-exponent-laws",
        "cnm_h039",
        "cnm_h042"
      ],
      "explanation": "Both sides contain a power of \\(9\\), so rewrite the larger power to expose the common quantity. Because the base is positive, the exponent law gives \\(9^{x+2}=9^x\\cdot9^2=81\\cdot9^x\\). Hence\n\\[\n81\\cdot9^x=240+9^x,\n\\qquad\n80\\cdot9^x=240,\n\\qquad\n9^x=3.\n\\]\nNow \\(3=\\sqrt9=9^{1/2}\\). Since \\(9>1\\), the function \\(9^x\\) is strictly increasing, so equal powers of this base have equal exponents. Therefore \\(x=\\tfrac12=\\boxed{0.5}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
      "id": "isolate-the-common-power",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1957_AMC_12_Q33#isolate-the-common-power",
      "title": "Isolate the common power"
    },
